        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Burns, Kids and Pets: Where a Warm Mist Unit Belongs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important thing to understand about a boiling humidifier is that it contains water at or near boiling point and releases vapor hot enough to burn skin at the outlet. That is not a design flaw; it is how the unit works. The risks come from three directions: a child or pet knocking it over, someone putting a hand or face over the outlet, and steam condensing on a nearby surface that someone touches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement handles most of it. Put the unit on a flat, stable, waterproof surface at least a couple of feet off the floor and out of a walkway. Keep it at least a few feet from a bed or crib so nobody can reach it while half asleep, and away from curtains, wallpaper and wooden furniture that will take the condensation. Do not run the cord across a path where someone can catch it with a foot and pull the unit down.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pediatric advice is consistent: the American Academy of Pediatrics and the Mayo Clinic both point parents toward cool mist humidifiers for children because hot water and steam can cause burns. If you have toddlers in the house, the safest warm mist option is not to have one in their room at all. A cooled-output design like the Boneco S250 reduces the risk from the plume, but the chamber is still hot and the unit can still be tipped.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pets bring the same tipping risk plus curiosity. Cats in particular will investigate a warm object with a moving plume. A shelf or dresser top they cannot reach, or a room they are not allowed in, is the realistic answer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No warm mist within reach of anyone under five.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a child can touch it, tip it or pull its cord, use a cool mist humidifier in that room instead. Every other precaution is a fallback for when this one is not possible.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finally, respect automatic shut-off. All six of our picks turn off when the water runs low, and you should never defeat that by propping a tank or running the unit without one. Unplug it before filling or moving it, and let it cool before you empty the chamber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
