          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the math with stated assumptions. Take a 280-watt unit running flat out for eight hours a night. That is 2.24 kilowatt-hours. At an electricity price of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, close to the recent US residential average, that comes to about 38 cents a night, or roughly $11.40 over a 30-night month. Run the same unit for 16 hours a day and you are looking at around $23 a month. A 30-watt ultrasonic unit doing the same eight hours costs about four cents a night.
          </p>

