        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Warm Mist Unit in a Child's Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pediatric guidance from the American Academy of Pediatrics and the Mayo Clinic favors cool mist for children because a tipped warm mist humidifier can scald. If a child can reach it, tip it or pull its cord, use a cool mist unit in that room. A cooled-output steam design lowers the risk from the plume but not from the hot chamber.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Weekly Descale</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boiling units leave every mineral in your tap water in the heating chamber. In hard-water areas the crust builds within weeks, slowing heating, raising energy use and eventually killing the element. A weekly vinegar soak, done the way the manual describes, takes ten minutes and keeps the unit working for seasons rather than months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Hybrid to Prevent White Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The warm mode on an ultrasonic hybrid heats water but still mists it through the transducer, so dissolved minerals reach the air just as they do in cool mode. If you have hard water and want no dust, use distilled water in a hybrid or buy a true boiling unit instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring Oils or Inhalants Into the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Essential oils and inhalant liquids can crack plastic, clog parts and void the warranty when added to the water. Use only the medicine cup or aroma slot your unit provides, with the product it was designed for, and follow that product's label, including its age limits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Boiling Unit Without a Hygrometer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Simple vaporizers like the Vicks units have no humidistat and run until the tank is empty. In a small, closed bedroom that can push humidity past 60 percent overnight, inviting condensation and mold. A cheap hygrometer and a target of 30 to 50 percent prevent it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a warm mist humidifier better than cool mist?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better overall. Warm mist boils or heats the water, which makes it more hygienic and keeps minerals out of the air in boiling designs, but it uses far more electricity and can cause burns. Cool mist is cheaper to run and safer around children. Hybrids let you switch between the two depending on the weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does a warm mist humidifier use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most warm mist units are rated somewhere between about 100 and 300 watts. At 280 watts running eight hours a night and 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is roughly $11 a month in the worst case. Units with a humidistat cycle the heater off and use less. Ultrasonic cool mist units typically draw 25 to 40 watts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do warm mist humidifiers make a room warmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not noticeably. The vapor cools within inches of the outlet, and the heat involved is small compared with a space heater. What changes is how the air feels: humid air at the same temperature feels a little warmer to many people than dry air, which is a comfort benefit rather than heating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are warm mist humidifiers safe for babies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pediatric guidance generally says no. The American Academy of Pediatrics and the Mayo Clinic recommend cool mist humidifiers for children because hot water and steam can cause burns if the unit is tipped or touched. Use a cool mist humidifier in a nursery and keep any warm mist unit out of children's reach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use tap water in a warm mist humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a boiling or steam unit, yes. Minerals stay behind as scale, so you will need to descale regularly, but they do not become white dust. In an ultrasonic hybrid, tap water will produce white dust in both modes, so distilled water is the better choice. Check your manual, since some electrode vaporizers need minerals to work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I clean a warm mist humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Empty and rinse it daily, descale the heating chamber weekly with white vinegar or the solution your manual specifies, and disinfect as a separate step when the manual recommends it. Never mix bleach and vinegar. At the end of the season, clean everything, dry it completely and store it open.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put Vicks or essential oils in my humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only in the medicine cup or aroma slot designed for it, with the product the maker specifies. Vicks units are designed for VapoSteam in the medicine cup. Never add oils or inhalants to the water tank; they can damage plastic and parts and may void the warranty. Follow the inhalant's label for age limits.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

